{"parentUuid":"ac4f1177-6601-4794-9e96-9bb80d2cace1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqyGScARgBJ4bB3kuAc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Wa5BwkKoNgZbTS5D9gkVRy","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md; echo ======; cat _scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt","description":"Read review and scope file"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32265,"cache_read_input_tokens":18241,"output_tokens":162,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":23},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32265},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":162,"cache_read_input_tokens":18241,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32265,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Wa5BwkKoNgZbTS5D9gkVRy":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md; echo ======; cat _scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt","description":"Read review and scope file"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqyG8WTLEWg914f4Qwv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"07d379a4-e491-45ef-9f24-0e7019bbde24","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:12.747Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"07d379a4-e491-45ef-9f24-0e7019bbde24","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n# Review: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (10 entries), Opus independent reviewer\n\nDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 404-414 (printed 393-403), plus PDF 175 (printed 164), PDF 21-22 (printed 10-11), PDF 315 (printed 304) and PDF 335-336 (printed 324-325) for cross-references. PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nHow checked: full text layer of PDF 404-414, 175, 21, 22, 315, 335-336. Page images opened for PDF 405, 406 (300 dpi, plus three full-resolution crops of Fig 7.2), 408, 409, 410, 411, 412, 413, 414 and 175 (renders in `staging/_renders/r7a_p*.png`). Every number quoted in the entries was matched against the page image as well as the text layer, because the text layer garbles some of them (\"3*6 sets\", \"1*3 repetitions\", \"I RM\", \"I Classical\" for \"A. Classical\"). All 13 \"Eric's baseline\" quotes were checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 30, 41, 61, 76, 103, 104, 107, 109, 119, 142, 180, 182 and 186. Quotes, line numbers and provenance tags all match. Every `[[wikilink]]` in the 10 files resolves to a file in the vault or the staging library.\n\n## Confidence rule applied to all 10\n\nThe brief sets `supported` as the ceiling for training prescriptions and `untested` for anecdotes. The folder applies this unevenly. Table 1.1 (`framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal`, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\") and DeLorme/Oxford are `supported`. The Ch5 circuit-layout catalogue is `untested`. This review uses one rule:\n\n- **`supported`**: a prescription with parameters that the book presents as established practice in the strength sports, such as \"one of the best known\", \"commonly used\" or \"popularly used\". This matches the Table 1.1 entry.\n- **`untested`**: a single anecdote; a bodybuilding novelty the authors themselves discount; a list of tips given without basis; or a taxonomy whose only empirical claim is an unevidenced ordering.\n- **`contested`**: only where the entry shows a same-question conflict, and with the reason scoped to the claim actually in conflict.\n\n| Entry | Current | Deserves | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Figs 7.1 and 7.2 chart | untested | untested (keep) | A taxonomy. Its one empirical claim is the \"increasing importance of nervous system training\" arrow, which has no evidence (PDF 405). The deficit rule is rated `contested` in its own linked entry. |\n| Maximal / supramaximal / circa-max / submax | contested | contested (keep, scope it) | The definitions themselves would be `supported`. Keeping `contested` follows the Ch1 lesson, provided the reason is scoped to the one rest figure (see FIX). |\n| Three bodybuilding regimes | untested | **supported** | Parameterised prescriptions the book calls \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", with add-ons \"popularly used\" (PDF 408). Same footing as Table 1.1. |\n| Set structures | untested | **supported** | Parameterised prescriptions. Plateau is \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), pyramids carry %1RM values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409), and DeLorme/APRE (rated `supported` and `contested` elsewhere) are named as pyramid forms. |\n| Interval training and cluster sets | contested | contested (keep) | A real same-paragraph conflict on cluster load, and stronger than the entry shows (see FIX). |\n| Hybrid repetitions and sets | untested | untested (keep) | The examples are the authors' own constructions (\"may be devised by the inventive coach\", PDF 411). No practice base or data is given beyond the clean-and-jerk. |\n| Progressive and random segmental | untested | untested (keep) | One anecdote (Rigert) with no outcome reported. |\n| Contrast methods | contested | contested (keep, scope it to heavy-light) | See FIX. |\n| Miscellaneous bodybuilding sets | untested | untested (keep) | The authors frame these as novelties \"of minimal significance\" for sport (PDF 412). |\n| 27 sticking-point strategies | untested | untested (keep) | A list of tips (\"Among some of the methods are the following\", PDF 413) with no basis given for most items. |\n\n## Voice\n\nNo first-person Siff passage and no third-person \"Verkhoshansky\" passage appears anywhere on PDF 404-414. The \"you\" in the sticking-point list (PDF 413-414) and in Peak Contraction Sets (PDF 413) is instructional second person, not authorial first person. All 10 entries therefore correctly write \"the authors\", as the brief requires when a passage cannot be assigned to one author. No entry presents Eric's or the writer's view as the authors'. The attribution problems listed below are unlabelled writer inferences.\n\n## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: Fig 7.1 boxes, caption and arrow direction against the\n\n... [11846 characters truncated] ...\n\nhecked: PDF 413-414 images. Items 1-18 run on PDF 413 into 414, then 20-28. There is no item 19, so the list holds 27. The entry's six groups contain every one of the 27 numbers exactly once (1,2,3,7 / 4,6,8 / 5,13 / 9,11,20,21,26,27,28 / 10,15,16,17,18,25 / 12,14,22,23,24). The entry paraphrases with short quotes and does not reprint the list. Checked \"80 - 92.5 - 97.5 - 102.5kg\", \"60 - 80 - 100kg\", \"0.5 - 1.25kg\", the item 12 competition caveat, the item 13 rationale, and the opening quote including the sic \"one of single most\". Baseline lines 30, 61 and 142 quoted and tagged correctly. The Ch5 overtraining caution is cited to its own entry and labelled as not from this list. |\n\nCounts: **GO 3, FIX 7, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Duplicates (check 5)\n\nNone. Grepped the vault library and the staging library for: cluster, heavy-light, rest-pause, pyramid, twenty-one / segmental, hybrid, sticking point, peripheral heart / PHA, drop set / stripping, superset, pre-exhaust, superslow. The relevant hits cover different angles and are already linked from these entries:\n\n- circuit layouts (superset / stripping circuits)\n- parachute contrast release\n- APRE and DeLorme pyramids\n- Setchinov station sequencing\n- Zatsiorsky's joint-angle \"sticking point\"\n- the Ch3 interval-style energy cost\n\nThe existing CAT entry already covers Compensatory Deceleration and Compensatory Action Training from PDF 411, so leaving CAT out of this batch is correct.\n\n## Missing (check 6)\n\n1. **Quasi-isometrics as a discrete \"superslow\" method** (PDF 405 / printed 394). Quasi-isometrics is unavoidable with near-maximal loads, but as a chosen method it means very slow repetitions with moderate or light loads, \"popularised in bodybuilding circles as the superslow method\". No library entry mentions superslow. It could be a short addition to the Fig 7.1/7.2 entry, which already lists Quasi-isometrics as a box.\n2. **Connective tissue first, plus low-intensity cyclical work in general preparation** (PDF 405 / printed 394). \"Great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues ... particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases\", and low-intensity prolonged cyclical activity is \"an essential part of the general preparatory phase of all training\", also useful for restoration. This is a programming rule, not a method, and no entry in this batch captures it. Check `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` for overlap before writing a new entry.\n3. **Heavy full-range resistance as active stretching** (PDF 405). \"Several of the other training methods with heavy resistance used over a full range of movement constitute very effective means of active stretching.\" This could be one line in the chart entry or a link from a flexibility entry.\n4. **Concluding scope statement** (PDF 414 / printed 403). The summary covers \"the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\", and leaves out magazine methods that \"differ only in name and a few minor details\". This is useful context for the confidence of the whole 7.1 catalogue: the authors present it as frequently used practice. It could go in `_book.md` or the chart entry.\n5. **Chapter opening on knowledge sources** (PDF 404 / printed 393). It lists five sources (weightlifting and powerlifting, bodybuilding, supplementary resistance training, physical therapy, scientific research) and says science only recently began testing Iron Game practice. This explains why most of 7.1 rests on practice, not studies. It is low priority, and one sentence in the chart entry would cover it.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. **Maximal methods:** scope the `contested` reason to the single rest figure, add the \"between sets\" against cluster-context caveat, and drop or correct \"forced reps\" as a higher-risk method with a stated warning.\n2. **Three regimes:** set `confidence: supported`, and label the split-routine/zone sentence as Inference.\n3. **Set structures:** set `confidence: supported`, and label the step-method purpose and \"not for a peaking block\" as Inference.\n4. **Interval and cluster:** add the direct same-paragraph contradictions (4-6RM; the 10-20% strip) to the `contested` reason.\n5. **Hybrid:** label the time/space clause, and resolve the superscript with the PDF 336 / printed 325 convention (it is a set count).\n6. **Segmental:** restate the random variant's use as preventive (PDF 410), or label the stall use as Inference.\n7. **Contrast:** scope `contested` to the heavy-light set (not load release or pneumatics), and report the Russian positive findings from the same PDF 175 page in both \"Why `contested`\" and \"When NOT to apply\".\n\nFixes 2 and 3 apply the confidence rule stated above. If Eric prefers to keep catalogue entries at `untested`, apply that consistently to the `supported` Table 1.1 entry as well, rather than leaving the folder split.\n(eval):1: ===== not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wa5BwkKoNgZbTS5D9gkVRy"}]},"uuid":"0414491f-6d8e-4050-a6a5-f75e98ca0006","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:12.853Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Exit code 1\n# Review: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (10 entries), Opus independent reviewer\n\nDate: 2026-10-03. Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. Source: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 404-414 (printed 393-403), plus PDF 175 (printed 164), PDF 21-22 (printed 10-11), PDF 315 (printed 304) and PDF 335-336 (printed 324-325) for cross-references. PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nHow checked: full text layer of PDF 404-414, 175, 21, 22, 315, 335-336. Page images opened for PDF 405, 406 (300 dpi, plus three full-resolution crops of Fig 7.2), 408, 409, 410, 411, 412, 413, 414 and 175 (renders in `staging/_renders/r7a_p*.png`). Every number quoted in the entries was matched against the page image as well as the text layer, because the text layer garbles some of them (\"3*6 sets\", \"1*3 repetitions\", \"I RM\", \"I Classical\" for \"A. Classical\"). All 13 \"Eric's baseline\" quotes were checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 30, 41, 61, 76, 103, 104, 107, 109, 119, 142, 180, 182 and 186. Quotes, line numbers and provenance tags all match. Every `[[wikilink]]` in the 10 files resolves to a file in the vault or the staging library.\n\n## Confidence rule applied to all 10\n\nThe brief sets `supported` as the ceiling for training prescriptions and `untested` for anecdotes. The folder applies this unevenly. Table 1