{"parentUuid":"4d7451a8-b8cf-4f3b-af66-f1e0bc51b020","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqySwNvxBUjo8JSmvDN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BG8B9SCdbAFHSCPtRthdM1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; sed -n '/## Entry verdicts/,/Counts:/p' review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md; echo XXXX; cat _scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt","description":"Read entry verdicts and scope"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8606,"cache_read_input_tokens":50506,"output_tokens":166,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8606},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":166,"cache_read_input_tokens":50506,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8606,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BG8B9SCdbAFHSCPtRthdM1":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; sed -n '/## Entry verdicts/,/Counts:/p' review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md; echo XXXX; cat _scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt","description":"Read entry verdicts and scope"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffqySBV7Smr2WRN3eKZ3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f7419b9c-7a2f-4d12-99c2-3f7ace428060","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:14.770Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f7419b9c-7a2f-4d12-99c2-3f7ace428060","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BG8B9SCdbAFHSCPtRthdM1","type":"tool_result","content":"## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: Fig 7.1 boxes, caption and arrow direction against the PDF 405 image. Fig 7.2 at 300 dpi plus crops (PDF 406): all four column headers; 8 quality boxes, with connectors only from Explosive Strength (to Reactive), Flexibility (to the static/passive/active/ballistic/PNF box) and Hypertrophy (to Bodybuilding Methods), so \"five of eight have no drawn link\" is correct; Reactive connects to both Plyometrics and Contrast; Supramaximal runs straight to its variations box; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal have no connector; Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods have no variations; Oxford Method floats unlinked; Weightlifting, Isometrics, Pyramiding, Bodybuilding, Circuit and Multi-purpose each connect to their variation boxes; Stripping appears twice and APRE twice; every variation list matches. Quotes at PDF 404-405 / printed 393-394 and the concluding remark at PDF 414 / printed 403. Chapter 1 deficit quotes at PDF 21 / printed 10, including \"5RM to 8RM\". Baseline lines 76 and 103 quoted and tagged correctly. Rating `untested` is consistent with the rule above. |\n| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **FIX** | (1) The \"Why `contested`\" paragraph says \"Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training\". PDF 406 frames its 1-5 min as \"This 1RM set with optimal recovery intervals **between sets**\". The PDF 409 sentence (\"single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\") opens the interval-training section and leads straight into Cluster Training (\"10-20 second interval between each repetition ... in an extended set\", PDF 409). So the two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles in one extended set). The conflict is real as worded, but it is not certain they answer the same question. (2) The entry carries `contested` because of one rest figure, but the entry is four categories of definitions, so a reader filtering by confidence would read the load bands as disputed. (3) \"When NOT to apply\" says \"the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407)\". PDF 407 gives an injury warning for eccentrics (\"carries an increased risk of injury\") and for cheating (\"if injury is to be avoided\"), but none for forced repetitions. | (1) After the first sentence of \"Why `contested`\", add: \"PDF 406 calls the 1-5 minutes 'optimal recovery intervals between sets', and the PDF 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which.\" Change \"Both sentences describe\" to \"As worded, both sentences describe\". (2) Start that paragraph with: \"The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute.\" (3) Change to \"the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group\" or delete \"forced reps\". |\n| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **FIX** | (1) Confidence `untested` understates. These are parameterised prescriptions the book calls \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\" and \"popularly used\" (PDF 408 / printed 397), the same basis as the `supported` Table 1.1 entry. (2) \"When to apply\": \"Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\" The authors say only that zone training \"must be spread over several sessions or days\" (PDF 409). The sentence sits after an \"Inference:\" sentence, so its label scope is unclear. Everything else checks: the table values (3-5 x 8-10, 2-5 min; 15-20 at 60-70%, 2-3 min; 5-8 at 80-90%, 2-5 min), the \"A.\" label (image), Table 1.1's 60-80 / 8-15 / 4-8 / 2-5 (PDF 22 / printed 11), the supersets, pre-exhaustion and split-week quotes, and baseline lines 61, 104 and 180. | (1) Set `confidence: supported`. (2) Prefix: \"Inference: split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\" |\n| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **FIX** | (1) Confidence `untested` understates, as above. Plateau is \"commonly used\" (PDF 408) and the pyramid values are \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF 409). (2) Unlabelled writer claims: \"Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target\" (\"When to apply\"), and \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block\" (\"When NOT to apply\"). The authors give the plateau's use (PDF 408) but say nothing about peaking, and give no purpose for the step method. All numbers check against the PDF 408-409 images: 50x10/70x6 x3, 60-85 x3, the 5-10 kg strip, \"no more than 6 repetitions\", both pyramids, 2-6 min, 4 to 10, 2.5-5 kg, the \"rarely used by serious athletes\" quote and item 7 (PDF 413). | (1) Set `confidence: supported`. (2) Rewrite as \"Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target\" and \"Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.\" |\n| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **FIX** | The contested reason is correct but incomplete, and it leans on the weaker evidence. The same paragraph that sets \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM\" (PDF 409 / printed 398, image) also prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's **4-6RM**\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping variant where \"the load is decreased by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF 409-410). Both fall below the stated minimum in the text itself, without any %1RM conversion. The entry's 75-90% argument depends on reading the pyramid's \"6 x 85%\" as a 6RM, an inference (correctly labelled). All other values check against the PDF 409-410 images: 30-40% / 5-6 x 20-30 / 1-2 min; 50-60% / 3-6 sets to failure / 10-90 s; \">180 beats/min\"; circuits 1-3 min; \"75% of (220-Age)\". The Ch5 comparison (20-40 reps at ≤30% against 3-8 reps at >70%) checks at PDF 315 / printed 304. Baseline lines 119 and 182 are correct. | In \"Why `contested`\", lead with the direct text: \"The same paragraph sets the minimum at 'one's 5RM' (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering 'with one's 4-6RM' and a stripping form that cuts the load 'by 10-20%' each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives '75-90% of 1RM' (PDF p. 410).\" Keep the existing pyramid-based Inference after it as supporting reasoning. |\n| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **FIX** (minor) | (1) \"When to apply — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar\". The authors say only \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF 410). Time and space are the writer's addition and are unlabelled. (2) The superscript is left \"Unclear as printed ... Inference: the superscript may be a set count\". The book settles this itself: the 6.6.2 convention writes 80 kg x 6 done twice as \"6(80)²\", with the superscript as the set count, and gives the hybrid example \"100 / 2+3+1\" (PDF 336 / printed 325). The PDF 411 image shows 80³ over 3+5+2, so the notation means 3 sets, and the book's \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" is a slip in its own wording. All other content checks against the PDF 410-411 images, including both example lists and the two guidelines. | (1) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the time/space clause, or replace it with \"When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (PDF p. 410).\" (2) Replace the unclear note with: \"By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF p. 336 / printed p. 325, where '6(80)²' means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's 'for a set of 3 repetitions' conflicts with its own convention.\" |\n| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **FIX** (minor) | \"When to apply\": \"The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled.\" The authors give the random variant a preventive purpose, \"to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime\" (PDF 410 / printed 399, image), not a remedy for a stall. The sentence sits outside the preceding \"Inference:\" sentence. All quotes check against the PDF 410 image, including \"2-4 segments\", \"3-7 repetitions\", Twenty-Ones, Rigert's three segments of 1-3 reps, and \"Frequently ... full clean pull\". | Replace with: \"The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors' stated purpose, PDF p. 410).\" If the stall use is wanted, write it as \"Inference: ...\". |\n| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **FIX** | (1) Scope. The entry says \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute.\" The 3.4.2 Western studies test \"the speed of an unloaded movement ... directly after the same movement was executed with a weight\" (PDF 175 / printed 164, image). That is a preceding loaded effort followed by a lighter one, which is the heavy-light set. Load release and pneumatics drop the load inside one repetition. The authors give a different mechanism for them (\"the contrast in stimulus ... produces a rapid acceleration\", PDF 411), and the cited studies did not test it. (2) The evidence is reported one-sided. The same page, PDF 175, reports the Russian side: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system ... and an improvement in speed and strength\" (Portnov 1955 and others), and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises ... in shotputting ... and in rowing\". \"When NOT to apply\" says only \"the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks\". That is the one-sided reading the Ch1 lesson forbids. All method definitions and the 20% / 10-20 s / 3-5 rep figures check against the PDF 411 image, and the Ch5 shock-method link (contrast methods listed, \"unsuitable for novices\") checks. | (1) In \"Why `contested`\", change \"The heavy-light set and load release rest on\" to \"The heavy-light set rests on\". Add: \"Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.\" (2) Add after the Western list: \"The same page reports Russian findings in favour: heavy dynamic work 'elicits a positive after-effect ... and an improvement in speed and strength', and intense preliminary strength tension improves jumping, shot-put and rowing results (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164).\" In \"When NOT to apply\", change the first sentence to: \"Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book reports Western studies that found none and Russian studies that found one (PDF p. 175).\" |\n| framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md | **GO** | None found. | checked: the \"Miscellaneous Methods\" heading heads PDF 412 (image), followed by the frame quote (\"of minimal significance\", \"shock the body ... out of any