it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"108e390c-fc4a-48d1-bb98-c4ea418d22a7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ab43fe92-94b5-4037-933a-26ba83c19e3d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffqyu6kJvHou57tPPK6y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PCgYbY7aQMS9bHpJ8zg9nx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md; do echo \"#### $f\"; cat -n $f; done","description":"Read first three fix entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1702,"cache_read_input_tokens":72497,"output_tokens":393,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":55},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1702},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":393,"cache_read_input_tokens":72497,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1702,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1702,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PCgYbY7aQMS9bHpJ8zg9nx":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md; do echo \"#### $f\"; cat -n $f; done","description":"Read first three fix entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffqytqd3KXu5XjY9T9ty","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fad0112f-00e0-41ed-8e24-1977edd4c936","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:22.475Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fad0112f-00e0-41ed-8e24-1977edd4c936","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCgYbY7aQMS9bHpJ8zg9nx","type":"tool_result","content":"#### framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: methods by load category\n     8\tcategory: [strength, methods, maximal-strength, loading]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Maximal, Supramaximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods: Definitions, Loads and Rests\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 7.1 defines four load categories of resistance method (PDF pp. 406-407 / printed pp. 395-396, checked against the page images):\n    17\t\n    18\t**Maximal methods**\n    19\t- *Weightlifting method:* \"single repetition explosive sets or low repetition sets (2-3 reps), in which each repetition is separated from the next by a short rest period (1-5 minutes, depending on how close the load is to a 1RM)\", also called \"the *interval set* method\" (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Bodybuilders adopted it as the *rest-pause method*, \"sets of 1RM, but with rest periods of 10-20 seconds\"; the authors say the shorter rest \"is possible, since bodybuilding movements do not require the high levels of skill, speed and coordination of the Olympic lifts\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n    20\t- *Maximal or circa-maximal concentrics:* the concentric phase of a 1RM or near-1RM lift is completed and the bar is dropped (bumper plates) \"so as to avoid executing the depleting eccentric lowering phase\"; in bodybuilding \"a partner usually assists in the lowering\" (PDF p. 407).\n    21\t- *Maximal isometrics:* slow or explosive isometrics \"in single or few repetition sets lasting about 5 seconds with rest intervals of 2-3 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 407).\n    22\t- *Maximal eccentrics:* a load \"about 30-40% greater than one's maximum concentric lift\" taken from racks or raised by a partner and lowered under control (PDF p. 407).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Supramaximal methods** (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396)\n    25\t- *Forced repetitions:* to failure, then \"a few more repetitions with the aid of a training partner, who provides the minimal assistance necessary\" for the concentric phase; the eccentric phase \"is carried out without help\".\n    26\t- *Restricted range repetitions:* a load heavier than the full-range maximum, moved through \"a few small range concentric-eccentric repetitions near his strongest region of action\"; done at the end of \"a normal 5-8 repetition\" set to the point of \"burn\", these are \"*burning reps*\". Weightlifters and powerlifters use partial ranges \"to rectify any deficit of strength in that region\", for example pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses at the \"sticking point\", set up in power racks.\n    27\t- *Cheating:* momentum, bouncing, swaying or accessory muscles, used above 1RM or at the end of a heavy set to failure; \"There are recommended safe ways of cheating and these must be followed religiously if injury is to be avoided\" (the safe ways are not described).\n    28\t- *Ballistics:* \"lowering or dropping a weight rapidly to recruit the myotatic stretch reflex or exploit the stored elastic energy\"; \"the training load is not necessarily supramaximal; rather, it is the resulting muscle tension that reaches this level of intensity.\"\n    29\t\n    30\t**Circa-maximal methods:** loads \"characteristically about 90-97.5% of 1RM\"; the difference from maximal methods is that circa-maximal work \"may be performed with several successive repetitions without a prolonged rest interval\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Submaximal methods:** \"loads of about 60-80% of one's 1RM to the point of failure on the last set\", \"mainly for bodybuilding reasons such as hypertrophy or muscle definition\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Classification inconsistency (not a contradiction).** Maximal eccentrics are described under Maximal Methods in the text, which adds that \"Often the eccentric method is regarded as one of the 'supramaximal' methods\" (PDF p. 407); Fig 7.2 lists eccentrics under supramaximal (PDF p. 406, image). The method is the same; only its heading differs.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]].\n    39\t\n    40\t**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the 7.5-point width of the circa-maximal band may be no larger than the error of a projected max, so that band may be easier to hit by repetitions in reserve or bar speed than by a percentage. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n    41\t\n    42\t**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Example** — The authors' own examples: partial pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses in the range of the \"sticking point\", with power racks securing the start position (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396); and a weightlifter dropping a near-1RM bar on bumper plates to skip the eccentric phase (PDF p. 407).\n    45\t\n    46\t**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (Chapter 5's maximum-strength methods and loads); [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]] (forced reps, restricted range maxima and maximal eccentrics as shock methods); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]] (why partial-range work may transfer narrowly); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]; [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]\n#### framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: bodybuilding regimes and set methods\n     8\tcategory: [hypertrophy, bodybuilding, sets-and-reps, programming]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Three Bodybuilding Regimes (Classical, Extensive, Intensive) and the Add-on Methods They Use\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under Submaximal Methods the authors give three regimes (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397, checked against the page image):\n    17\t\n    18\t| Regime | Sets x reps | Load | Rest between sets |\n    19\t|---|---|---|---|\n    20\t| A. Classical | 3-5 x 8-10, same load | not stated | 2-5 min |\n    21\t| B. Extensive (\"high volume\") | 3-5 x 15-20 | \"about 60-70%\" | 2-3 min, \"increasing with increase in load from set to set\" |\n    22\t| C. Intensive (\"high intensity\") | 3-5 x 5-8 | \"80-90%\" | 2-5 min, increasing with load |\n    23\t\n    24\tThe classical regime \"may also be regarded as a particular case of the *plateau system*\". A second classical form raises the load each set so that repetitions fall \"from 10 in the first set to 4-5 repetitions in the last set\", with rest rising as load rises: \"a typical form of the *pyramid system*\". For the intensive regime the authors point to \"Figs 1.8 and 1.9\" (not checked for this entry).\n    25\t\n    26\t\"Each of the preceding regimes may use\" forced repetitions, cheating, eccentrics (negatives), burning repetitions and ballistics (defined in [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]), plus these \"popularly used\" methods (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397):\n    27\t- **Supersets:** set 1 of exercise 1, then set 1 of exercise 2, and so on. \"Since supersets are not intended to develop strength, the athlete allows little or no rest between sets.\" More than two exercises is *multi-setting*; the *giant set* is \"4-6 different exercises performed in a mini-circuit\".\n    28\t- **Pre-exhaustion:** a single-joint exercise first \"preferentially exhausts one of the muscle groups that will be used in the following multi-joint exercise\", which also \"compels other muscles to act strongly as accessory muscles\".\n    29\t- **Repetitions to failure:** until no further repetition is possible without help; \"often used for muscle endurance and definition training\".\n    30\t- **Split routine:** two components trained on successive workouts (upper then lower body; or flexors and adductors one day, extensors and abductors the next); advanced athletes may split within a day. \"A common weekly split routine prescribes split training on Monday-Tuesday, resting on Wednesday, more split training on Thursday-Friday, completed by a weekend of passive or active rest.\"\n    31\t- **Zone training** (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398): one body zone per session, so a whole-body regime \"must be spread over several sessions or days\"; split routines are \"one variation of this method\".\n    32\t\n    33\t**Set against Table 1.1.** The book's own goal chart gives hypertrophy as 60-80% 1RM, 8-15 repetitions, 4-8 sets, 2-5 min rest (PDF p. 22 / printed p. 11, per [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]). The three regimes overlap it on load and rest; the extensive regime's 15-20 repetitions and the intensive regime's 5-8 repetitions fall outside 8-15, and all three use 3-5 sets against 4-8. Inference: these are named variants inside a broad goal chart, not two answers to one question, so this is recorded as a difference, not a contradiction. The submaximal category itself is \"about 60-80% of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 407), while the intensive regime reaches 90%.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for supersets (little rest because they \"are not intended to develop strength\") and for pre-exhaustion (the pre-exhausted prime mover forces accessory muscles to work harder, PDF p. 408). They give no mechanism for the regimes and no study. Chapter 7 places these methods under the structural aim of hypertrophy ([[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]); Chapter 1 sets out the kinds of hypertrophy they produce ([[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]).\n    36\t\n    37\t**When to apply** — When a hypertrophy or work-capacity phase is planned and the coach wants named, bounded regimes. Eric's baseline (line 180): \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps ...)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: the classical and extensive regimes sit inside those course bands; the intensive regime's 80-90% sits above the course's hypertrophy band, in the range the course gives for the strength phase (\"strength phase 80-90% intensity\", same line). Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\n    38\t\n    39\t**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes.\n    40\t\n    41\t**Example** — The authors' own: \"if supine dumbbell flyes are performed before a bench press, the pectoral and anterior deltoid muscles are pre-exhausted and the triceps are forced to become more vigorously involved\" (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397).\n    42\t\n    43\t**Related** — [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (plateau and pyramid systems); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]] (the book's view on how far a single-joint exercise isolates a muscle); [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (a two-station circuit is a superset)\n#### framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: set structures and pyramids\n     8\tcategory: [sets-and-reps, loading, programming, bodybuilding]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Set Structures: Plateau, Step, Stripping, Three Kinds of Pyramid, and the Reverse Pyramid\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors define six ways of arranging load and repetitions across the sets of one exercise (PDF pp. 408-409 / printed pp. 397-398, checked against the page images):\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Plateau:** \"After a specific warmup with a light weight, the load remains constant for 3-4 sets (e.g. 50 x 10, 70 x 6, 70 x 6, 70 x 6)\". Used \"during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases after weeks of heavy training\" (PDF p. 408). The unit of 50 and 70 is not given.\n    19\t- **Step:** \"The number of repetitions is kept fixed, while the load is increased after each set until the prescribed number of repetitions can no longer be maintained (e.g. 60 x 3, 70 x 3, 80 x 3, 85 x 3)\" (PDF p. 408).\n    20\t- **Stripping (descending set, \"Drop Sets\"):** pyramid up \"until a target load is reached with about 3-4 repetitions\", then \"A partner immediately removes 5-10 kg and the athlete exercises to failure\", repeated \"until the starting weight or less is reached\". Used \"for muscle endurance and definition training\". If the rising pyramid reaches \"a 1RM or 2RM with no more than 6 repetitions for any set, then stripping offers a logical way of developing strength and muscle endurance, since the use of higher repetition sets is too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408).\n    21\t- **Pyramid (load pyramiding):** \"4-5 sets\" with load rising and repetitions falling. Submaximal example \"10 x 70%.1RM, 8 x 80%.1RM, 6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM\"; maximal strength example \"6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM, 2 x 97%.1RM, 1 x 100%.1RM\", with the values stated to be \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (not checked for this entry). Rest \"2-6 minutes, depending on the load used, the aim of the pyramid and the number of the set (the rest increases with load)\" (PDF pp. 408-409).\n    22\t- **Repetition pyramiding:** fixed load, repetitions raised \"by one or two repetitions per set (e.g. from an initial 4 to a maximum of 10)\". **Rest pyramiding:** fixed load, rest progressively shortened; load is rarely raised at the same time, and if it is, \"the increments are kept small (2.5-5 kg)\" (PDF p. 409). The DeLorme method and APRE \"involve some form of pyramiding\" (PDF p. 409).\n    23\t- **Reverse pyramid:** pyramid up to a final set, then \"work down the pyramid in reverse order using the same number of repetitions for each load\". It is \"very similar to the stripping method, but does not require the athlete to exercise to failure\" (PDF p. 409).\n    24\t\n    25\tThe authors then add: \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today; they find it more beneficial to progress rapidly to the main training load and performing higher repetition work on the way down from a maximum, if absolutely necessary\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). Their sticking-point list repeats this: \"If you uses pyramids, use fewer repetition sets to reach your maximum, then do your repetition work during a reverse pyramid down from your maximum. This can be especially useful if you wish to concurrently work on strength and hypertrophy\" (item 7, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n    26\t\n    27\tInference: this sits alongside, not against, the chapter's opening point that pyramiding can serve hypertrophy, strength or endurance \"in the earlier preparatory stages\" (PDF p. 405). One sentence says what pyramiding can do; the other says what serious athletes prefer.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Why it works** — The one reason the authors give is for stripping and the reverse pyramid: high-repetition sets on the way up are \"too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408), so the repetition work goes after the heavy set. They give no mechanism or study for the other structures.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead.\n    32\t\n    33\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Example** — The authors' own maximal-strength pyramid: 6 x 85%, 4 x 90%, 2 x 97%, 1 x 100% of 1RM, with rest lengthening as the load rises, inside 2-6 minutes (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n    36\t\n    37\t**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]] (the classical regime as a plateau case, the second classical method as a pyramid); [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (pyramid and stripping circuits); [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"36284028-e71a-4bdb-9c44-2ed4a710905f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:22.679Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"#### framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: methods by load category\n     8\tcategory: [strength, methods, maximal-strength, loading]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Maximal, Supramaximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods: Definitions, Loads and Rests\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 7.1 defines four load categories of resistance method (PDF pp. 406-407 / printed pp. 395-396, checked against the page images):\n    17\t\n    18\t**Maximal methods**\n    19\t- *Weightlifting method:* \"single repetition explosive sets or low repetition sets (2-3 reps), in which each repetition is separated from the next by a short rest period (1-5 minutes, depending on how close the load is to a 1RM)\", also called \"the *interval set* method\" (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Bodybuilders adopted it as the *rest-pause method*, \"sets of 1RM, but with rest periods of 10-20 seconds\"; the authors say the shorter rest \"is possible, since bodybuilding movements do not require the high levels of skill, speed and coordination of the Olympic lifts\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n    20\t- *Maximal or circa-maximal concentrics:* the concentric phase of a 1RM or near-1RM lift is completed and the bar is dropped (bumper plates) \"so as to avoid executing the depleting eccentric lowering phase\"; in bodybuilding \"a partner usually assists in the lowering\" (PDF p. 407).\n    21\t- *Maximal isometrics:* slow or explosive isometrics \"in single or few repetition sets lasting about 5 seconds with rest intervals of 2-3 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 407).\n    22\t- *Maximal eccentrics:* a load \"about 30-40% greater than one's maximum concentric lift\" taken from racks or raised by a partner and lowered under control (PDF p. 407).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Supramaximal methods** (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396)\n    25\t- *Forced repetitions:* to failure, then \"a few more repetitions with the aid of a training partner, who provides the minimal assistance necessary\" for the concentric phase; the eccentric phase \"is carried out without help\".\n    26\t- *Restricted range repetitions:* a load heavier than the full-range maximum, moved through \"a few small range concentric-eccentric repetitions near his strongest region of action\"; done at the end of \"a normal 5-8 repetition\" set to the point of \"burn\", these are \"*burning reps*\". Weightlifters and powerlifters use partial ranges \"to rectify any deficit of strength in that region\", for example pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses at the \"sticking point\", set up in power racks.\n    27\t- *Cheating:* momentum, bouncing, swaying or accessory muscles, used above 1RM or at the end of a heavy set to failure; \"There are recommended safe ways of cheating and these must be followed religiously if injury is to be avoided\" (the safe ways are not described).\n    28\t- *Ballistics:* \"lowering or dropping a weight rapidly to recruit the myotatic stretch reflex or exploit the stored elastic energy\"; \"the training load is not necessarily supramaximal; rather, it is the resulting muscle tension that reaches this level of intensity.\"\n    29\t\n    30\t**Circa-maximal methods:** loads \"characteristically about 90-97.5% of 1RM\"; the difference from maximal methods is that circa-maximal work \"may be performed with several successive repetitions without a prolonged rest interval\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Submaximal methods:** \"loads of about 60-80% of one's 1RM to the point of failure on the last set\", \"mainly for bodybuilding reasons such as hypertrophy or muscle definition\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Classification inconsistency (not a contradiction).** Maximal eccentrics are described under Maximal Methods in the text, which adds that \"Often the eccentric method is regarded as one of the 'supramaximal' methods\" (PDF p. 407); Fig 7.2 lists eccentrics under supramaximal (PDF p. 406, image). The method is the same; only its heading differs.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]].\n    39\t\n    40\t**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the 7.5-point width of the circa-maximal band may be no larger than the error of a projected max, so that band may be easier to hit by repetitions in reserve or bar speed than by a percentage. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n    41\t\n    42\t**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Example** — The authors' own examples: partial pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses in the range of the \"sticking point\", with power racks securing the start position (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396); and a weightlifter dropping a near-1RM bar on bumper plates to skip the eccentric phase (PDF p. 407).\n    45\t\n    46\t**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (Chapter 5's maximum-strength methods and loads); [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]] (forced reps, restricted range maxima and maximal eccentrics as shock methods); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]] (why partial-range work may transfer narrowly); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]; [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]\n#### framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: bodybuilding regimes and set methods\n     8\tcategory: [hypertrophy, bodybuilding, sets-and-reps, programming]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Three Bodybuilding Regimes (Classical, Extensive, Intensive) and the Add-on Methods They Use\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under Submaximal Methods the authors give three regimes (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397, checked against the page image):\n    17\t\n    18\t| Regime | Sets x reps | Load | Rest between sets |\n    19\t|---|---|---|---|\n    20\t| A. Classical | 3-5 x 8-10, same load | not stated | 2-5 min |\n    21\t| B. Extensive (\"high volume\") | 3-5 x 15-20 | \"about 60-70%\" | 2-3 min, \"increasing with increase in load from set to set\" |\n    22\t| C. Intensive (\"high intensity\") | 3-5 x 5-8 | \"80-90%\" | 2-5 min, increasing with load |\n    23\t\n    24\tThe classical regime \"may also be regarded as a particular case of the *plateau system*\". A second classical form raises the load each set so that repetitions fall \"from 10 in the first set to 4-5 repetitions in the last set\", with rest rising as load rises: \"a typical form of the *pyramid system*\". For the intensive regime the authors point to \"Figs 1.8 and 1.9\" (not checked for this entry).\n    25\t\n    26\t\"Each of the preceding regimes may use\" forced repetitions, cheating, eccentrics (negatives), burning repetitions and ballistics (defined in [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]), plus these \"popularly used\" methods (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397):\n    27\t- **Supersets:** set 1 of exercise 1, then set 1 of exercise 2, and so on. \"Since supersets are not intended to develop strength, the athlete allows little or no rest between sets.\" More than two exercises is *multi-setting*; the *giant set* is \"4-6 different exercises performed in a mini-circuit\".\n    28\t- **Pre-exhaustion:** a single-joint exercise first \"preferentially exhausts one of the muscle groups that will be used in the following multi-joint exercise\", which also \"compels other muscles to act strongly as accessory muscles\".\n    29\t- **Repetitions to failure:** until no further repetition is possible without help; \"often used for muscle endurance and definition training\".\n    30\t- **Split routine:** two components trained on successive workouts (upper then lower body; or flexors and adductors one day, extensors and abductors the next); advanced athletes may split within a day. \"A common weekly split routine prescribes split training on Monday-Tuesday, resting on Wednesday, more split training on Thursday-Friday, completed by a weekend of passive or active rest.\"\n    31\t- **Zone training** (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398): one body zone per session, so a whole-body regime \"must be spread over several sessions or days\"; split routines are \"one variation of this method\".\n    32\t\n    33\t**Set against Table 1.1.** The book's own goal chart gives hypertrophy as 60-80% 1RM, 8-15 repetitions, 4-8 sets, 2-5 min rest (PDF p. 22 / printed p. 11, per [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]). The three regimes overlap it on load and rest; the extensive regime's 15-20 repetitions and the intensive regime's 5-8 repetitions fall outside 8-15, and all three use 3-5 sets against 4-8. Inference: these are named variants inside a broad goal chart, not two answers to one question, so this is recorded as a difference, not a contradiction. The submaximal category itself is \"about 60-80% of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 407), while the intensive regime reaches 90%.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for supersets (little rest because they \"are not intended to develop strength\") and for pre-exhaustion (the pre-exhausted prime mover forces accessory muscles to work harder, PDF p. 408). They give no mechanism for the regimes and no study. Chapter 7 places these methods under the structural aim of hypertrophy ([[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]); Chapter 1 sets out the kinds of hypertrophy they produce ([[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]).\n    36\t\n    37\t**When to apply** — When a hypertrophy or work-capacity phase is planned and the coach wants named, bounded regimes. Eric's baseline (line 180): \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps ...)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: the classical and extensive regimes sit inside those course bands; the intensive regime's 80-90% sits above the course's hypertrophy band, in the range the course gives for the strength phase (\"strength phase 80-90% intensity\", same line). Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\n    38\t\n    39\t**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes.\n    40\t\n    41\t**Example** — The authors' own: \"if supine dumbbell flyes are performed before a bench press, the pectoral and anterior deltoid muscles are pre-exhausted and the triceps are forced to become more vigorously involved\" (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397).\n    42\t\n    43\t**Related** — [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (plateau and pyramid systems); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]] (the book's view on how far a single-joint exercise isolates a muscle); [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (a two-station circuit is a superset)\n#### framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: set structures and pyramids\n     8\tcategory: [sets-and-reps, loading, programming, bodybuilding]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Set Structures: Plateau, Step, Stripping, Three Kinds of Pyramid, and the Reverse Pyramid\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors define six ways of arranging load and repetitions across the sets of one exercise (PDF pp. 408-409 / printed pp. 397-398, checked against the page images):\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Plateau:** \"After a specific warmup with a light weight, the load remains constant for 3-4 sets (e.g. 50 x 10, 70 x 6, 70 x 6, 70 x 6)\". Used \"during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases after weeks of heavy training\" (PDF p. 408). The unit of 50 and 70 is not given.\n    19\t- **Step:** \"The number of repetitions is kept fixed, while the load is increased after each set until the prescribed number of repetitions can no longer be maintained (e.g. 60 x 3, 70 x 3, 80 x 3, 85 x 3)\" (PDF p. 408).\n    20\t- **Stripping (descending set, \"Drop Sets\"):** pyramid up \"until a target load is reached with about 3-4 repetitions\", then \"A partner immediately removes 5-10 kg and the athlete exercises to failure\", repeated \"until the starting weight or less is reached\". Used \"for muscle endurance and definition training\". If the rising pyramid reaches \"a 1RM or 2RM with no more than 6 repetitions for any set, then stripping offers a logical way of developing strength and muscle endurance, since the use of higher repetition sets is too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408).\n    21\t- **Pyramid (load pyramiding):** \"4-5 sets\" with load rising and repetitions falling. Submaximal example \"10 x 70%.1RM, 8 x 80%.1RM, 6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM\"; maximal strength example \"6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM, 2 x 97%.1RM, 1 x 100%.1RM\", with the values stated to be \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (not checked for this entry). Rest \"2-6 minutes, depending on the load used, the aim of the pyramid and the number of the set (the rest increases with load)\" (PDF pp. 408-409).\n    22\t- **Repetition pyramiding:** fixed load, repetitions raised \"by one or two repetitions per set (e.g. from an initial 4 to a maximum of 10)\". **Rest pyramiding:** fixed load, rest progressively shortened; load is rarely raised at the same time, and if it is, \"the increments are kept small (2.5-5 kg)\" (PDF p. 409). The DeLorme method and APRE \"involve some form of pyramiding\" (PDF p. 409).\n    23\t- **Reverse pyramid:** pyramid up to a final set, then \"work down the pyramid in reverse order using the same number of repetitions for each load\". It is \"very similar to the stripping method, but does not require the athlete to exercise to failure\" (PDF p. 409).\n    24\t\n    25\tThe authors then add: \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today; they find it more beneficial to progress rapidly to the main training load and performing higher repetition work on the way down from a maximum, if absolutely necessary\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). Their sticking-point list repeats this: \"If you uses pyramids, use fewer repetition sets to reach your maximum, then do your repetition work during a reverse pyramid down from your maximum. This can be especially useful if you wish to concurrently work on strength and hypertrophy\" (item 7, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n    26\t\n    27\tInference: this sits alongside, not against, the chapter's opening point that pyramiding can serve hypertrophy, strength or endurance \"in the earlier preparatory stages\" (PDF p. 405). One sentence says what pyramiding can do; the other says what serious athletes prefer.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Why it works** — The one reason the authors give is for stripping and the reverse pyramid: high-repetition sets on the way up are \"too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408), so the repetition work goes after the heavy set. They give no mechanism or study for the other structures.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead.\n    32\t\n    33\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Example** — The authors' own maximal-strength pyramid: 6 x 85%, 4 x 90%, 2 x 97%, 1 x 100% of 1RM, with rest lengthening as the load rises, inside 2-6 minutes (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n    36\t\n    37\t**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]] (the classical regime as a plateau case, the second classical method as a pyramid); [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (pyramid and stripping circuits); [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fad0112f-00e0-41ed-8e24-1977edd4c936","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
