{"parentUuid":"e3dcaddb-2d78-4784-9740-4e23ed5a62d4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffr1fBeDZJ2oaKVgxtJe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013TTRwZ3Yj9Vczqn79bzAZL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md; do echo \"#### $f\"; cat -n $f; done","description":"Read remaining four fix entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8601,"cache_read_input_tokens":100719,"output_tokens":553,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":311},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8601},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":553,"cache_read_input_tokens":100719,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8601,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013TTRwZ3Yj9Vczqn79bzAZL":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md; do echo \"#### $f\"; cat -n $f; done","description":"Read remaining four fix entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffr1cq1enwMQRaQDDqR1","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1bf8d20d-3faa-413d-9a46-7dd73dd0a793","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:48.913Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1bf8d20d-3faa-413d-9a46-7dd73dd0a793","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013TTRwZ3Yj9Vczqn79bzAZL","type":"tool_result","content":"#### framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: interval resistance and cluster sets\n     8\tcategory: [strength-endurance, interval-training, cluster-sets, loading]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Interval Resistance Training (Extensive and Intensive) and Cluster Sets\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors define interval resistance training by analogy with track: \"the primary aim of interval training is generally to develop strength-endurance by means of systematically alternating work and recovery phases\", with rest \"(preferably active rest)\" too short for full recovery \"so that the athlete is forced to cope with increasing exhaustion\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, checked against the page image). They split it into two broad categories, \"one to increase strength or power and the other to increase endurance\", and the endurance category into two methods (PDF p. 409):\n    17\t\n    18\t| | Extensive interval | Intensive interval |\n    19\t|---|---|---|\n    20\t| Develops | \"medium duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity | \"short-duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity |\n    21\t| Load | \"30-40% of 1RM\" | \"50-60% of 1RM\" |\n    22\t| Sets x reps | \"5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions\" | \"3-6 sets, where each set is performed to failure\" |\n    23\t| Rest | \"1-2 minutes\" | \"10-90 seconds\", by exercise, load, fitness and loading duration |\n    24\t| Energy | below 40% \"arterial blood flow is not restricted\" and \"'aerobic' energy processes predominate\" | higher loads \"inhibit arterial blood flow and anaerobic energy processes predominate\" |\n    25\t| Heart rate | large muscle groups needed to reach the level \"recommended for aerobic conditioning, e.g. 75% of (220-Age)\" | \"can readily increase heart rates to over 180 beats/min\" |\n    26\t| Circuits | \"Specially designed circuit training programmes\" can serve | special circuits with \"1-3 minutes\" between circuits |\n    27\t\n    28\tFor extensive work the authors want \"the larger muscle groups such as the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae\"; \"bicep curls, calf raises and tricep push-downs play a minimal role in elevating the heart rate\" (PDF p. 409).\n    29\t\n    30\t**Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.\n    33\t\n    34\tA second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].\n    35\t\n    36\t**Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: incomplete rest forces the athlete \"to cope with increasing exhaustion and thereby develop muscular endurance\"; below about 40% of maximum arterial flow is not restricted, so aerobic processes predominate; at 50-60% flow is inhibited and anaerobic processes predominate (PDF p. 409). They give no mechanism for cluster sets beyond classing them as interval training. Chapter 3 adds that a series of single repetitions costs more energy than a continuous set with the same load ([[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]).\n    39\t\n    40\t**When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict.\n    41\t\n    42\t**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).\n    43\t\n    44\t**Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n    45\t\n    46\t**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]; [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (rest-pause and the weightlifting interval set); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (stripping); [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n#### framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: hybrid exercises and sets\n     8\tcategory: [exercise-selection, complexes, weightlifting, programming]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Hybrid Training: Several Movements in One Repetition, or a Different Movement Within One Set\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"Hybrid Training ... refers to exercise in which each repetition consists of two or more separate movements between the starting and end points\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The clean-and-jerk, \"a pull from the ground, with a front squat, followed by a thrust overhead\", is \"probably the best known hybrid exercise\". The method also covers \"the *hybrid set*, which comprises a different movement for each repetition of the set\" (PDF p. 410).\n    17\t\n    18\tThe authors' examples (PDF p. 410):\n    19\t- Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye).\n    20\t- Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.\n    21\t- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count.\n    22\t\n    23\tGuidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n    26\t\n    27\t**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.\n    28\t\n    29\t**When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]\n#### heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: segmental range training\n     8\tcategory: [partial-range, weightlifting, exercise-variation, technique]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Progressive and Random Segmental Training: Work the Range in Segments, as Rigert Did for the Pull\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — *Progressive Segmental Training* divides \"the range of movement into 2-4 segments, then performing 3-7 repetitions in each successive segment\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The bodybuilding form is \"Twenty-Ones\": 7 repetitions over each third of a curl's range, \"(or frequently, over the full range of movement)\" for the last seven. The authors' weightlifting example: the Russian middle-heavyweight Rigert \"used this method for training the pull in the clean and the snatch\". In the clean pull \"the bar first was raised to just below the knees for 1-3 repetitions, next the bar was moved for 1-3 repetitions between this point and mid-thigh, and, finally, 1-3 repetitions were performed between mid-thigh and waist level with full shoulder shrug and extension onto the toes. Frequently, the set was completed with a full clean pull from the ground\" (PDF p. 410).\n    17\t\n    18\tA bodybuilding variant exercises the segments \"in a different or random order or pattern every set or workout to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\"; the authors name it \"Random Segmental Training\" \"For want of a better term\" (PDF p. 410).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for the random variant: varying the order limits \"early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\" (PDF p. 410). For the progressive form they give no mechanism. Inference: each segment of the pull can be practised with repetitions before the whole pull is joined up, which is how Rigert's set ends. Zatsiorsky's library entry on accommodation states the general law the random variant appeals to ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 2 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (restricted range repetitions); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]; [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]\n#### framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: reactive contrast methods\n     8\tcategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n    19\t- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n    20\t- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n    21\t- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n    22\t\n    23\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n    26\t\n    27\t**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n    28\t\n    29\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n    30\t\n    31\t**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n    32\t\n    33\t**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1170d6eb-0356-4c65-8f0d-9706f6e3ff6c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:49.127Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"#### framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: interval resistance and cluster sets\n     8\tcategory: [strength-endurance, interval-training, cluster-sets, loading]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Interval Resistance Training (Extensive and Intensive) and Cluster Sets\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors define interval resistance training by analogy with track: \"the primary aim of interval training is generally to develop strength-endurance by means of systematically alternating work and recovery phases\", with rest \"(preferably active rest)\" too short for full recovery \"so that the athlete is forced to cope with increasing exhaustion\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, checked against the page image). They split it into two broad categories, \"one to increase strength or power and the other to increase endurance\", and the endurance category into two methods (PDF p. 409):\n    17\t\n    18\t| | Extensive interval | Intensive interval |\n    19\t|---|---|---|\n    20\t| Develops | \"medium duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity | \"short-duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity |\n    21\t| Load | \"30-40% of 1RM\" | \"50-60% of 1RM\" |\n    22\t| Sets x reps | \"5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions\" | \"3-6 sets, where each set is performed to failure\" |\n    23\t| Rest | \"1-2 minutes\" | \"10-90 seconds\", by exercise, load, fitness and loading duration |\n    24\t| Energy | below 40% \"arterial blood flow is not restricted\" and \"'aerobic' energy processes predominate\" | higher loads \"inhibit arterial blood flow and anaerobic energy processes predominate\" |\n    25\t| Heart rate | large muscle groups needed to reach the level \"recommended for aerobic conditioning, e.g. 75% of (220-Age)\" | \"can readily increase heart rates to over 180 beats/min\" |\n    26\t| Circuits | \"Specially designed circuit training programmes\" can serve | special circuits with \"1-3 minutes\" between circuits |\n    27\t\n    28\tFor extensive work the authors want \"the larger muscle groups such as the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae\"; \"bicep curls, calf raises and tricep push-downs play a minimal role in elevating the heart rate\" (PDF p. 409).\n    29\t\n    30\t**Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.\n    33\t\n    34\tA second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].\n    35\t\n    36\t**Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: incomplete rest forces the athlete \"to cope with increasing exhaustion and thereby develop muscular endurance\"; below about 40% of maximum arterial flow is not restricted, so aerobic processes predominate; at 50-60% flow is inhibited and anaerobic processes predominate (PDF p. 409). They give no mechanism for cluster sets beyond classing them as interval training. Chapter 3 adds that a series of single repetitions costs more energy than a continuous set with the same load ([[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]).\n    39\t\n    40\t**When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict.\n    41\t\n    42\t**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).\n    43\t\n    44\t**Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n    45\t\n    46\t**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]; [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (rest-pause and the weightlifting interval set); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (stripping); [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n#### framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: hybrid exercises and sets\n     8\tcategory: [exercise-selection, complexes, weightlifting, programming]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Hybrid Training: Several Movements in One Repetition, or a Different Movement Within One Set\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"Hybrid Training ... refers to exercise in which each repetition consists of two or more separate movements between the starting and end points\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The clean-and-jerk, \"a pull from the ground, with a front squat, followed by a thrust overhead\", is \"probably the best known hybrid exercise\". The method also covers \"the *hybrid set*, which comprises a different movement for each repetition of the set\" (PDF p. 410).\n    17\t\n    18\tThe authors' examples (PDF p. 410):\n    19\t- Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye).\n    20\t- Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.\n    21\t- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count.\n    22\t\n    23\tGuidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n    26\t\n    27\t**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.\n    28\t\n    29\t**When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]\n#### heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: segmental range training\n     8\tcategory: [partial-range, weightlifting, exercise-variation, technique]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Progressive and Random Segmental Training: Work the Range in Segments, as Rigert Did for the Pull\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — *Progressive Segmental Training* divides \"the range of movement into 2-4 segments, then performing 3-7 repetitions in each successive segment\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The bodybuilding form is \"Twenty-Ones\": 7 repetitions over each third of a curl's range, \"(or frequently, over the full range of movement)\" for the last seven. The authors' weightlifting example: the Russian middle-heavyweight Rigert \"used this method for training the pull in the clean and the snatch\". In the clean pull \"the bar first was raised to just below the knees for 1-3 repetitions, next the bar was moved for 1-3 repetitions between this point and mid-thigh, and, finally, 1-3 repetitions were performed between mid-thigh and waist level with full shoulder shrug and extension onto the toes. Frequently, the set was completed with a full clean pull from the ground\" (PDF p. 410).\n    17\t\n    18\tA bodybuilding variant exercises the segments \"in a different or random order or pattern every set or workout to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\"; the authors name it \"Random Segmental Training\" \"For want of a better term\" (PDF p. 410).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for the random variant: varying the order limits \"early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\" (PDF p. 410). For the progressive form they give no mechanism. Inference: each segment of the pull can be practised with repetitions before the whole pull is joined up, which is how Rigert's set ends. Zatsiorsky's library entry on accommodation states the general law the random variant appeals to ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 2 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (restricted range repetitions); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]; [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]\n#### framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.1\"\n     7\ttopic: reactive contrast methods\n     8\tcategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n    19\t- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n    20\t- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n    21\t- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n    22\t\n    23\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n    26\t\n    27\t**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n    28\t\n    29\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n    30\t\n    31\t**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n    32\t\n    33\t**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1bf8d20d-3faa-413d-9a46-7dd73dd0a793","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
