meostasis|quasi'; grep -il superslow -r library /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library | head","description":"Read PDF 414 and 404, find overlap entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffr2k66pjynKVKTaRTQu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0e483837-18c5-4bea-ae60-a03c76652be6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:21:06.375Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0e483837-18c5-4bea-ae60-a03c76652be6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MciU15GoHig5gG3YAMkmGi","type":"tool_result","content":"######## PDF 414\nSUPERTRAINING\n13. Ensure that your rest periods between sets or repetitions are optimal. Rests which are either too short or too \nlong both may disrupt your ability to perform optimally, so it is essential that you experiment or apply \nappropriate tests (e.g. heart rate, motor control) to \ndetermine rest intervals which best suit you. In \ncompetition, however, the rules or number of competitors may not allow you optimal rest between attempts, \nso that it is vital that you train with different rest intervals to leam to cope with such situations.\n14. Take a short break from your regular training to overcome mental stagnation and facilitate restoration.\n15. Use fewer exercises in your workout\n16. Include more variation in your workout.\n17. Use shorter, more frequent workouts\n18. Subdivide your workouts into rational modules of a few exercises, then do two or more modules per day.\n20. Use ballistic methods to move through a sticking point in an exercise.\n21. Perform loaded isometric holds at crucial points\n22. Include formal restoration in your training programme, if you do not already do so\n23. Implement appropriate mental strategies by consulting qualified specialists\n24. Take a short break from the problem exercise or event\n25. Implement a basic periodisation programme or at least an portion of one, if you arc not already following one.\n26. Use assistance exercises to strengthen areas of weakness in the problem exercise\n27. Use ‘forced repetitions* with the aid of a training partner (details given earlier in Ch 7.1)\n28. Use variable resistance methods such as free weights combined with elastics or chains.\nConcluding Remarks\nThe above summary by no means exhausts the repertoire of methods and variations of resistance training that is \nused in resistance training, but it serves as a rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used \nin the training of high-level athletes. It intentionally docs not address several other methods quoted or ’invented' \nby writers in popular bodybuilding magazines, simply because they differ only in name and a few minor details \nfrom existing methods and do not constitute any major new contribution to the field of strength training.\nIn applying any of the methods discussed in this section, it is essential that the detailed information \nconccming their mechanisms and use given in earlier chapters be consulted in conjunction with the above \nand neuromuscular training, such as electrostimuiation, accelerated recuperation and PNF (proprioceptive \nneuromuscular facilitation). Because of the broad scope of training offered by PNF, this system is discussed in \ndetail later in this chapter.\nAs has been stressed throughout this text, all strength training depends on two dominant factors (e.g. see Ch \nI), namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning. \nThus, \nstrength training may be categorised according to its \nmajor structural and functional aims relative to the \nimportance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1). The numerous methods and techniques of training may \nthen be added hierarchically below each of these major aims.\nIt is tempting to attempt to devise a single extensive flowchart to categorise and interrelate all of the better \nknown methods of resistance training, but the extent of overlap between the different methods makes the final \nresult logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable. Instead, it is simpler to organise the different methods \nas a loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships (Fig \n7.2). The merit of this type of chart is that it enables one to see at a glance the variety o f many of the methods \nwhich were discussed in previous chapters. Where necessary, any terminology and methods that did not appear \nexplicitly in the book are described later, so as to furnish the reader with a compendium of methods for practical \napplication.\nIn using the summary depicted in Figure 7.2, it is important to note that some of the training methods may be \nused to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest \nintervals. For example, pyramiding should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method, because it may also\n7 S '\n393\n\n######## PDF 405\nclaim--active-flexibility-correlates-more-with-sporting-proficiency-than-passive.md\nclaim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned.md\nclaim--olympic-lifters-flexible-and-combined-stretch-strength-boosts-sprint-speed.md\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md\nclaim--spurious-muscle-tension-limits-rom-independent-of-tissue-flexibility.md\nclaim--static-cyclic-stretching-increases-rom-via-stretch-tolerance-not-tissue-change.md\nclaim--stretch-shortening-history-dependence-of-isometric-force.md\nclaim--stretching-benefits-list-from-older-literature-review.md\nframework--flexibility-definition-and-rom-determinants.md\nframework--flexibility-speed-strength-endurance-qualities.md\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md\nframework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md\nframework--six-parameters-influencing-flexibility.md\nframework--soft-tissue-contribution-to-joint-stiffness-and-stretch-duration-type.md\nframework--stretch-reflex-types-and-neuromuscular-control-of-flexibility.md\nframework--stretching-matrix-system-reverse-of-contraction-action.md\nframework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic.md\nheuristic--ballistic-stretching-safe-with-gradual-progressive-protocol.md\nheuristic--dedicated-stretching-may-be-unnecessary-with-progressive-movement-preparation.md\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md\nheuristic--no-unsafe-stretch-only-unsafe-execution-for-individual.md\nheuristic--reflex-based-techniques-for-flexibility-training.md\nheuristic--tendon-stretching-must-preserve-elastic-energy-storage-capacity.md\nprinciple--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue.md\nprinciple--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"365bb29f-d6b3-48dd-ab71-337a67122fc6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:21:06.786Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## PDF 414\nSUPERTRAINING\n13. Ensure that your rest periods between sets or repetitions are optimal. Rests which are either too short or too \nlong both may disrupt your ability to perform optimally, so it is essential that you experiment or apply \nappropriate tests (e.g. heart rate, motor control) to \ndetermine rest intervals which best suit you. In \ncompetition, however, the rules or number of competitors may not allow you optimal rest between attempts, \nso that it is vital that you train with different rest intervals to leam to cope with such situations.\n14. Take a short break from your regular training to overcome mental stagnation and facilitate restoration.\n15. Use fewer exercises in your workout\n16. Include more variation in your workout.\n17. Use shorter, more frequent workouts\n18. Subdivide your workouts into rational modules of a few exercises, then do two or more modules per day.\n20. Use ballistic methods to move through a sticking point in an exercise.\n21. Perform loaded isometric holds at crucial points\n22. Include formal restoration in your training programme, if you do not already do so\n23. Implement appropriate mental strategies by consulting qualified specialists\n24. Take a short break from the problem exercise or event\n25. Implement a basic periodisation programme or at least an portion of one, if you arc not already following one.\n26. Use assistance exercises to strengthen areas of weakness in the problem exercise\n27. Use ‘forced repetitions* with the aid of a training partner (details given earlier in Ch 7.1)\n28. Use variable resistance methods such as free weights combined with elastics or chains.\nConcluding Remarks\nThe above summary by no means exhausts the repertoire of methods and variations of resistance training that is \nused in resistance training, but it serves as a rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used \nin the training of high-level athletes. It intentionally docs not address several other methods quoted or ’invented' \nby writers in popular bodybuilding magazines, simply because they differ only in name and a few minor details \nfrom existing methods and do not constitute any major new contribution to the field of strength training.\nIn applying any of the methods discussed in this section, it is essential that the detailed information \nconccming their mechanisms and use given in earlier chapters be consulted in conjunction with the above \nand neuromuscular training, such as electrostimuiation, accelerated recuperation and PNF (proprioceptive \nneuromuscular facilitation). Because of the broad scope of training offered by PNF, this system is discussed in \ndetail later in this chapter.\nAs has been stressed throughout this text, all strength training depends on two dominant factors (e.g. see Ch \nI), namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning. \nThus, \nstrength training may be categorised according to its \nmajor structural and functional aims relative to the \nimportance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1). The numerous methods and techniques of training may \nthen be added hierarchically below each of these major aims.\nIt is tempting to attempt to devise a single extensive flowchart to categorise and interrelate all of the better \nknown methods of resistance training, but the extent of overlap between the different methods makes the final \nresult logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable. Instead, it is simpler to organise the different methods \nas a loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships (Fig \n7.2). The merit of this type of chart is that it enables one to see at a glance the variety o f many of the methods \nwhich were discussed in previous chapters. Where necessary, any terminology and methods that did not appear \nexplicitly in the book are described later, so as to furnish the reader with a compendium of methods for practical \napplication.\nIn using the summary depicted in Figure 7.2, it is important to note that some of the training methods may be \nused to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest \nintervals. For example, pyramiding should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method, because it may also\n7 S '\n393\n\n######## PDF 405\nclaim--active-flexibility-correlates-more-with-sporting-proficiency-than-passive.md\nclaim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned.md\nclaim--olympic-lifters-flexible-and-combined-stretch-strength-boosts-sprint-speed.md\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md\nclaim--spurious-muscle-tension-limits-rom-independent-of-tissue-flexibility.md\nclaim--static-cyclic-stretching-increases-rom-via-stretch-tolerance-not-tissue-change.md\nclaim--stretch-shortening-history-dependence-of-isometric-force.md\nclaim--stretching-benefits-list-from-older-literature-review.md\nframework--flexibility-definition-and-rom-determinants.md\nframework--flexibility-speed-strength-endurance-qualities.md\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md\nframework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md\nframework--six-parameters-influencing-flexibility.md\nframework--soft-tissue-contribution-to-joint-stiffness-and-stretch-duration-type.md\nframework--stretch-reflex-types-and-neuromuscular-control-of-flexibility.md\nframework--stretching-matrix-system-reverse-of-contraction-action.md\nframework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic.md\nheuristic--ballistic-stretching-safe-with-gradual-progressive-protocol.md\nheuristic--dedicated-stretching-may-be-unnecessary-with-progressive-movement-preparation.md\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md\nheuristic--no-unsafe-stretch-only-unsafe-execution-for-individual.md\nheuristic--reflex-based-techniques-for-flexibility-training.md\nheuristic--tendon-stretching-must-preserve-elastic-energy-storage-capacity.md\nprinciple--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue.md\nprinciple--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e483837-18c5-4bea-ae60-a03c76652be6","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
