{"parentUuid":"a479412c-6c5c-4164-8928-eafe5b01d5d7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B9YtfK2BC7GHMC8WqkWHJd","type":"tool_result","content":"framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:26:requires acceleration; isokinetic devices are more accurately \"quasi-isokinetic,\" and\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:28:joint angle. \"Quasi-isometric\" action is defined separately: the slow, near-static muscle\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:44:precision: heavy squat/bench-style lifting performed slowly is \"quasi-isometric,\" a\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:54:a slow, heavy back squat as \"quasi-isometric\" (near-static but occurring across a range)\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:7:topic: quasi-isometric methods for active flexibility\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:8:category: [flexibility, quasi-isometric, starting-strength, programming]\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:14:# Eccentric Quasi-Isometrics: Loading a Joint Slowly Past Its Static Flexibility Limit to Build Active Range\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:16:**What it is** — Quasi-isometric activity (defined generally in\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:22:natural resistance patterns. Eccentric quasi-isometrics specifically use the load to force\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:26:repetitions that gradually increase ROM with each one. Both quasi-isometric modes can be\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:45:**When to apply** — Consider eccentric quasi-isometric loading (slow, controlled,\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:59:reps, would be an application of eccentric quasi-isometrics as described here, distinct\n\nclaim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates.md\nframework--determinants-of-strength-structural-functional.md\nframework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md\n\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.9.4\"\ntopic: function of training volume\ncategory: [volume, intensity, long-term-adaptation, connective-tissue, qualification]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n\n**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reaction, and then to the development of long-term adaptation of the body. Therefore, in each successive annual cycle athletes should execute larger volumes of loading (within optimal limits) to raise and ma\n\nVolume alone is not enough: \"the influence of the volume can be determined correctly only if one also takes into consideration the magnitude of the loading, its duration and intensity\" (PDF p. 369). Magnitude is set \"first of all, on the basis of the athlete's qualification\": the higher the qualification, the larger the yearly and stage volume, and the share of \"specialised loading\" grows (Panov 1975; Zhikharevich 1976; Vankov 1977; Matveyev 1977; Gilyazova 1978). The annual volume is \"established individually\" from the preceding stages; \"A more serious task\" is the optimal volume per month, s\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one quoted: prolonged homeostatic disturbance mobilises \"energy resources and plastic reserves\" and moves the body from an acute to a general, then long-term, adaptive reaction (PDF p. 369). For connective tissue they refer back to 1.6 and 1.7.2 and give no further mechanism here.\n16:**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functiona","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f99962b6-0f30-46dd-bea6-1ce1b4db84d7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:21:12.646Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:26:requires acceleration; isokinetic devices are more accurately \"quasi-isokinetic,\" and\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:28:joint angle. \"Quasi-isometric\" action is defined separately: the slow, near-static muscle\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:44:precision: heavy squat/bench-style lifting performed slowly is \"quasi-isometric,\" a\nframework--precise-muscle-action-terminology.md:54:a slow, heavy back squat as \"quasi-isometric\" (near-static but occurring across a range)\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:7:topic: quasi-isometric methods for active flexibility\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:8:category: [flexibility, quasi-isometric, starting-strength, programming]\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:14:# Eccentric Quasi-Isometrics: Loading a Joint Slowly Past Its Static Flexibility Limit to Build Active Range\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:16:**What it is** — Quasi-isometric activity (defined generally in\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:22:natural resistance patterns. Eccentric quasi-isometrics specifically use the load to force\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:26:repetitions that gradually increase ROM with each one. Both quasi-isometric modes can be\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:45:**When to apply** — Consider eccentric quasi-isometric loading (slow, controlled,\nheuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength.md:59:reps, would be an application of eccentric quasi-isometrics as described here, distinct\n\nclaim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates.md\nframework--determinants-of-strength-structural-functional.md\nframework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md\n\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.9.4\"\ntopic: function of training volume\ncategory: [volume, intensity, long-term-adaptation, connective-tissue, qualification]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n\n**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reaction, and then to the development of long-term adaptation of the body. Therefore, in each successive annual cycle athletes should execute larger volumes of loading (within optimal limits) to raise and ma\n\nVolume alone is not enough: \"the influence of the volume can be determined correctly only if one also takes into consideration the magnitude of the loading, its duration and intensity\" (PDF p. 369). Magnitude is set \"first of all, on the basis of the athlete's qualification\": the higher the qualification, the larger the yearly and stage volume, and the share of \"specialised loading\" grows (Panov 1975; Zhikharevich 1976; Vankov 1977; Matveyev 1977; Gilyazova 1978). The annual volume is \"established individually\" from the preceding stages; \"A more serious task\" is the optimal volume per month, s\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one quoted: prolonged homeostatic disturbance mobilises \"energy resources and plastic reserves\" and moves the body from an acute to a general, then long-term, adaptive reaction (PDF p. 369). For connective tissue they refer back to 1.6 and 1.7.2 and give no further mechanism here.\n16:**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functiona","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a479412c-6c5c-4164-8928-eafe5b01d5d7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
