lume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue.md | cut -c1-500 | head -5; cat -n framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","description":"Read chart entry and overlap content"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffr3qxLRTJrDSr8U6zjQ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f42123f0-7372-4b58-9efa-f119d6a2cc25","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:21:19.008Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f42123f0-7372-4b58-9efa-f119d6a2cc25","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lqs2nvdZM8LfN1iskip8jP","type":"tool_result","content":"don't move relative to each other (joint angle constant); more accurately called \"static.\"\n\"Isotonic\" (constant tension) is argued to be almost never actually true during real joint\nmovement, since tension changes with acceleration, deceleration, and reflex activation\nthroughout a range; European and Russian scientists prefer \"auxotonic\" (changing tension\nand length) or \"allodynamic\" instead. \"Isokinetic\" (constant velocity) is also argued to\nbe physically impossible over a full range, since starting from and returning to rest\nrequires acceleration; isokinetic devices are more accurately \"quasi-isokinetic,\" and\nisokinetic-machine torque output is typically much lower than isometric torque at the same\njoint angle. \"Quasi-isometric\" action is defined separately: the slow, near-static muscle\naction seen in heavy, near-maximal resistance training performed anywhere across a joint's\nrange (not fixed at one angle like true isometrics), relevant to maximal strength,\nhypertrophy, and active flexibility training rather than power or speed. (PDF pp. 60-64,\nprinted pp. 49-53.)\n6:chapter: \"1.6\"\n14:# Muscle Adapts in Days; Tendon, Ligament, and Bone Take Weeks to Months\n8:category: [volume, intensity, long-term-adaptation, connective-tissue, qualification]\n14:# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n16:**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reaction, and then to the development of long-term adaptation of the body. Therefore, in each successive\n20:**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one quoted: prolonged homeostatic disturbance mobilises \"energy resources and plastic reserves\" and moves the body from an acute to a general, then long-term, adaptive reaction (PDF p. 369). For connective tissue they refer back to 1.6 and 1.7.2 and give no further mechanism here.\n26:**Example** — The authors' own: gymnastics, weightlifting, high jumping and triple jumping, where joint injury \"occurs frequently\" if connective tissue has not been prepared by extensive moderate-intensity work before peak dynamic loads (PDF p. 369 / printed p. 358). Inference: a jumper's year would open with weeks of moderate-volume, lower-impact jumping and strength work before depth jumps.\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7\"\n     7\ttopic: classifying strength training methods\n     8\tcategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n    17\t\n    18\tFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image and 300 dpi crops) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n    19\t- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n    20\t- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal. Reactive Methods connects to Plyometrics and Contrast Methods; Supramaximal Methods connects straight to its box of variations; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods have no drawn connector to any method.\n    21\t- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods, both with no drawn variations; an Oxford Method box in the variations column with no drawn link; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n    22\t\n    23\tStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n    24\t\n    25\t**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n    26\t\n    27\tThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n    32\t\n    33\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n    34\t\n    35\t**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n    36\t\n    37\t**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"684e2feb-df64-4231-aa27-3079088ded39","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:21:19.236Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"don't move relative to each other (joint angle constant); more accurately called \"static.\"\n\"Isotonic\" (constant tension) is argued to be almost never actually true during real joint\nmovement, since tension changes with acceleration, deceleration, and reflex activation\nthroughout a range; European and Russian scientists prefer \"auxotonic\" (changing tension\nand length) or \"allodynamic\" instead. \"Isokinetic\" (constant velocity) is also argued to\nbe physically impossible over a full range, since starting from and returning to rest\nrequires acceleration; isokinetic devices are more accurately \"quasi-isokinetic,\" and\nisokinetic-machine torque output is typically much lower than isometric torque at the same\njoint angle. \"Quasi-isometric\" action is defined separately: the slow, near-static muscle\naction seen in heavy, near-maximal resistance training performed anywhere across a joint's\nrange (not fixed at one angle like true isometrics), relevant to maximal strength,\nhypertrophy, and active flexibility training rather than power or speed. (PDF pp. 60-64,\nprinted pp. 49-53.)\n6:chapter: \"1.6\"\n14:# Muscle Adapts in Days; Tendon, Ligament, and Bone Take Weeks to Months\n8:category: [volume, intensity, long-term-adaptation, connective-tissue, qualification]\n14:# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n16:**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reaction, and then to the development of long-term adaptation of the body. Therefore, in each successive\n20:**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one quoted: prolonged homeostatic disturbance mobilises \"energy resources and plastic reserves\" and moves the body from an acute to a general, then long-term, adaptive reaction (PDF p. 369). For connective tissue they refer back to 1.6 and 1.7.2 and give no further mechanism here.\n26:**Example** — The authors' own: gymnastics, weightlifting, high jumping and triple jumping, where joint injury \"occurs frequently\" if connective tissue has not been prepared by extensive moderate-intensity work before peak dynamic loads (PDF p. 369 / printed p. 358). Inference: a jumper's year would open with weeks of moderate-volume, lower-impact jumping and strength work before depth jumps.\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7\"\n     7\ttopic: classifying strength training methods\n     8\tcategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n    17\t\n    18\tFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image and 300 dpi crops) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n    19\t- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n    20\t- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal. Reactive Methods connects to Plyometrics and Contrast Methods; Supramaximal Methods connects straight to its box of variations; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods have no drawn connector to any method.\n    21\t- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods, both with no drawn variations; an Oxford Method box in the variations column with no drawn link; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n    22\t\n    23\tStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n    24\t\n    25\t**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n    26\t\n    27\tThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n    32\t\n    33\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n    34\t\n    35\t**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n    36\t\n    37\t**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f42123f0-7372-4b58-9efa-f119d6a2cc25","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
