{"parentUuid":"c1a82d5c-823e-4bf2-a119-7442b30dc0c9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrFhrWn6hivB514rDzs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15246,"cache_read_input_tokens":155186,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15246,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":34726,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffrFNUwNNfx14tncbegA","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ad516e04-a0f1-47d9-9375-9bc07f55ee78","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:29.876Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ad516e04-a0f1-47d9-9375-9bc07f55ee78","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrFhrWn6hivB514rDzs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E91E7Q563h9zJSbcz7yWSD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"mkdir -p /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp && cat > /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp/f7a_apply.py <<'PYEOF'\nimport pathlib\nD = pathlib.Path('/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky')\ndef rep(fn, pairs):\n    p = D/fn; t = p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')\n    for old, new in pairs:\n        n = t.count(old)\n        assert n == 1, (fn, n, old[:70])\n        t = t.replace(old, new)\n    p.write_text(t, encoding='utf-8'); print('ok', fn)\n\n# Fix 1\nrep('framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md', [\n ('**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives',\n  '**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute. The weightlifting method gives'),\n ('Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them.',\n  'As worded, both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training. PDF p. 406 calls the 1-5 minutes \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\", and the PDF p. 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which, and does not reconcile them.'),\n ('the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407)',\n  'the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group'),\n ('the book gives two incompatible values (above).',\n  'the book gives two values that may describe different methods (above).'),\n])\n# Fix 2\nrep('framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md', [\n ('confidence: untested', 'confidence: supported'),\n ('same line). Split routines and zone training suit', 'same line). Inference: split routines and zone training suit'),\n])\n# Fix 3\nrep('framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md', [\n ('confidence: untested', 'confidence: supported'),\n ('(PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target.',\n  '(PDF p. 408). Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target.'),\n ('Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.',\n  'Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.'),\n])\n# Fix 4 (+ consistency with fix 1)\nrep('framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md', [\n ('**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one\\'s 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference:',\n  '**Why `contested`.** The same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one\\'s 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one\\'s 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both of which take the load below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). All of these state the load for the same method. Inference:'),\n ('(PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].',\n  '(PDF p. 409). The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which; see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].'),\n ('without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM).',\n  'without choosing among its load statements (the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM, the 10-20% strip and 75-90% of 1RM).'),\n])\n# Fix 5\nrep('framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md', [\n ('Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count.',\n  'By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF pp. 335-336 / printed pp. 324-325, checked against the page images, where \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" is also written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\", so \"6(80)²\" means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text\\'s \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" conflicts with its own convention.'),\n ('**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors\\' use).',\n  '**When to apply** — When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors\\' use, PDF p. 410). Inference: also when session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar.'),\n ('push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).',\n  'push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar, written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention) (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).'),\n])\n# Fix 6\nrep('heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md', [\n ('The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled.',\n  'The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors\\' stated purpose, PDF p. 410).'),\n])\n# Fix 7\nrep('framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md', [\n ('**Why `contested`.** The book\\'s own 3.4.2 section',\n  '**Why `contested`.** The rating applies to the heavy-light set. The book\\'s own 3.4.2 section'),\n ('The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster.',\n  'The same page reports Russian findings in favour: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system (CNS), which produces a general toning influence on the motor apparatus and an improvement in speed and strength (Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957; Diachkov, 1961; Muravov & Tkachev, 1964; Letunov, 1965)\", and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises (Diachkov, 1958; 1961; Verkhoshansky, 1961; Tatian, 1964; Tkachev, 1967), in shotputting (Fritsch, 1961; Ivanova, 1964; Markov, 1966), and in rowing (Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966)\" (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against the page image). The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.'),\n ('Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book\\'s own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175).',\n  'Do not expect a reliable acute boost from the heavy-light set: the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one (PDF p. 175).'),\n])\n# Missing ideas -> chart entry\nrep('framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md', [\n ('see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\\n',\n  'see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\\n\\n'\n  '**Sources and scope of the catalogue.** The chapter opens with the sources of strength-training knowledge: \"Weightlifting and Powerlifting\", \"Bodybuilding\", \"Supplementary resistance training\", \"Physical therapy\" and \"Scientific research\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393, checked against the page image). \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously the foundations, validity and scope of the vast amount of practical information gathered by several generations of adherents of the \\'Iron Game\\'\" (PDF p. 404). The concluding remarks call 7.1 \"a rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\", and say it leaves out methods \"quoted or \\'invented\\' by writers in popular bodybuilding magazines, simply because they differ only in name and a few minor details from existing methods\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403, checked against the page image). Inference: most of the 7.1 catalogue rests on practice in the strength sports, not on studies cited on these pages.\\n\\n'\n  '**Notes beside Fig 7.1** (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, checked against the page image):\\n'\n  '- *Superslow.* Quasi-isometrics \"occurs as a natural consequence of training with near-maximal loads\", but \"there is a discrete method of quasi-isometrics (nowadays popularised in bodybuilding circles as the *superslow method*), which entails performing very slow controlled repetitions with even moderately heavy or lighter loads. In this case the athlete chooses to move the load slowly; with heavier weights he has no option but to move slowly.\" No load, repetition or rest figures are given. The term itself is defined in Chapter 1 ([[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]]).\\n'\n  '- *Active stretching.* \"several of the other training methods with heavy resistance used over a full range of movement constitute very effective means of active stretching\".\\n'\n  '- *Connective tissue first.* \"great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues, such as the tendons, ligaments, cartilages and joint capsules, particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases to levels of higher competitive intensity (see 1.6.2)\". Chapter 1 and Chapter 6 hold this point in [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]] and [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]].\\n'\n  '- *Low-intensity cyclical work.* \"An essential part of the general preparatory phase of all training is also low intensity, prolonged cyclical activity to enhance cardiovascular and circulatory processes, as well as capillarisation in all the relevant muscle groups. This same type of activity can also play a useful role in restoration between intensive training sessions and phases.\" No study, duration or intensity is given.\\n'),\n ('Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit',\n  'Do not read the catalogue as tested: the authors say science has only \"in recent years\" begun to examine Iron Game practice (PDF p. 404). Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit'),\n ('[[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)',\n  '[[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics); [[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]] (quasi-isometric defined); [[heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength]]; [[framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]] (connective tissue adapts slowly); [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]'),\n])\nPYEOF\ncd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && python3 staging/_tmp/f7a_apply.py","description":"Apply all fixes to 7a entries via exact replacement script"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15246,"cache_read_input_tokens":15518