aining (10-20 s within an extended set), so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which, and does not reconcile them.'),\n ('the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407)',\n  'the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group'),\n ('the book gives two incompatible values (above).',\n  'the book gives two values that may describe different methods (above).'),\n])\n# Fix 2\nrep('framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md', [\n ('confidence: untested', 'confidence: supported'),\n ('same line). Split routines and zone training suit', 'same line). Inference: split routines and zone training suit'),\n])\n# Fix 3\nrep('framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md', [\n ('confidence: untested', 'confidence: supported'),\n ('(PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target.',\n  '(PDF p. 408). Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target.'),\n ('Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.',\n  'Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block.'),\n])\n# Fix 4 (+ consistency with fix 1)\nrep('framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md', [\n ('**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one\\'s 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference:',\n  '**Why `contested`.** The same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one\\'s 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one\\'s 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both of which take the load below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). All of these state the load for the same method. Inference:'),\n ('(PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].',\n  '(PDF p. 409). The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which; see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].'),\n ('without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM).',\n  'without choosing among its load statements (the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM, the 10-20% strip and 75-90% of 1RM).'),\n])\n# Fix 5\nrep('framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md', [\n ('Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count.',\n  'By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF pp. 335-336 / printed pp. 324-325, checked against the page images, where \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" is also written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\", so \"6(80)²\" means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text\\'s \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" conflicts with its own convention.'),\n ('**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors\\' use).',\n  '**When to apply** — When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors\\' use, PDF p. 410). Inference: also when session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar.'),\n ('push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).',\n  'push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar, written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention) (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).'),\n])\n# Fix 6\nrep('heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md', [\n ('The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled.',\n  'The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors\\' stated purpose, PDF p. 410).'),\n])\n# Fix 7\nrep('framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md', [\n ('**Why `contested`.** The book\\'s own 3.4.2 section',\n  '**Why `contested`.** The rating applies to the heavy-light set. The book\\'s own 3.4.2 section'),\n ('The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster.',\n  'The same page reports Russian findings in favour: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system (CNS), which produces a general toning influence on the motor apparatus and an improvement in speed and strength (Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957; Diachkov, 1961; Muravov & Tkachev, 1964; Letunov, 1965)\", and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises (Diachkov, 1958; 1961; Verkhoshansky, 1961; Tatian, 1964; Tkachev, 1967), in shotputting (Fritsch, 1961; Ivanova, 1964; Markov, 1966), and in rowing (Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966)\" (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against the page image). The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly.'),\n ('Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book\\'s own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175).',\n  'Do not expect a reliable acute boost from the heavy-light set: the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one (PDF p. 175).'),\n])\n# Missing ideas -> chart entry\nrep('framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md', [\n ('see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\\n',\n  'see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\\n\\n'\n  '**Sources and scope of the catalogue.** The chapter opens with the sources of strength-training knowledge: \"Weightlifting and Powerlifting\", \"Bodybuilding\", \"Supplementary resistance training\", \"Physical therapy\" and \"Scientific research\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393, checked against the page image). \"Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously the foundations, validity and scope of the vast amount of practical information gathered by several generations of adherents of the \\'Iron Game\\'\" (PDF p. 404). The concluding remarks call 7.1 \"a rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes\", and say it leaves out methods \"quoted or \\'invented\\' by writers in popular bodybuilding magazines, simply because they differ only in name and a few minor details from existing methods\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403, checked against the page image). Inference: most of the 7.1 catalogue rests on practice in the strength sports, not on studies cited on these pages.\\n\\n'\n  '**Notes beside Fig 7.1** (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, checked against the page image):\\n'\n  '- *Superslow.* Quasi-isometrics \"occurs as a natural consequence of training with near-maximal loads\", but \"there is a discrete method of quasi-isometrics (nowadays popularised in bodybuilding circles as the *superslow method*), which entails performing very slow controlled repetitions with even moderately heavy or lighter loads. In this case the athlete chooses to move the load slowly; with heavier weights he has no option but to move slowly.\" No load, repetition or rest figures are given. The term itself is defined in Chapter 1 ([[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]]).\\n'\n  '- *Active stretching.* \"several of the other training methods with heavy resistance used over a full range of movement constitute very effective means of active stretching\".\\n'\n  '- *Connective tissue first.* \"great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues, such as the tendons, ligaments, cartilages and joint capsules, particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases to levels of higher competitive intensity (see 1.6.2)\". Chapter 1 and Chapter 6 hold this point in [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]] and [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]].\\n'\n  '- *Low-intensity cyclical work.* \"An essential part of the general preparatory phase of all training is also low intensity, prolonged cyclical activity to enhance cardiovascular and circulatory processes, as well as capillarisation in all the relevant muscle groups. This same type of activity can also play a useful role in restoration between intensive training sessions and phases.\" No study, duration or intensity is given.\\n'),\n ('Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit',\n  'Do not read the catalogue as tested: the authors say science has only \"in recent years\" begun to examine Iron Game practice (PDF p. 404). Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit'),\n ('[[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)',\n  '[[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics); [[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]] (quasi-isometric defined); [[heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength]]; [[framework--stretching-techniques-taxonomy-static-vs-dynamic]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]] (connective tissue adapts slowly); [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]'),\n])\nPYEOF\ncd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && python3 staging/_tmp/f7a_apply.py","description":"Apply all fixes to 7a entries via exact replacement script"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrFNUwNNfx14tncbegA","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"617844d6-552d-4d59-906a-569e0ad4e060","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:59.511Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"617844d6-552d-4d59-906a-569e0ad4e060","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E91E7Q563h9zJSbcz7yWSD","type":"tool_result","content":"ok framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md\nok framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md\nok framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md\nok framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md\nok framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md\nok heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md\nok framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md\nok framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4c69e24f-5011-4250-b4ce-48a10ec2fdf3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:59.928Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md\nok framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md\nok framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md\nok framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md\nok framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md\nok heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md\nok framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md\nok framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":true,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":11,"newStart":22,"newLines":11,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].","+**Why `contested`.** The rating applies to the heavy-light set. The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image). The same page reports Russian findings in favour: \"Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system (CNS), which produces a general toning influence on the motor apparatus and an improvement in speed and strength (Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957; Diachkov, 1961; Muravov & Tkachev, 1964; Letunov, 1965)\", and \"intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises (Diachkov, 1958; 1961; Verkhoshansky, 1961; Tatian, 1964; Tkachev, 1967), in shotputting (Fritsch, 1961; Ivanova, 1964; Markov, 1966), and in rowing (Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966)\" (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against the page image). The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]."," "," **When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).","+**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost from the heavy-light set: the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]])."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":18,"oldLines":16,"newStart":18,"newLines":16,"lines":[" The authors' examples (PDF p. 410):"," - Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye)."," - Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.","-- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count.","+- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF pp. 335-336 / printed pp. 324-325, checked against the page images, where \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\" is also written \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\", so \"6(80)²\" means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" conflicts with its own convention."," "," Guidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399)."," ","-**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.","+**When to apply** — When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410). Inference: also when session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar. Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not."," "," **When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here."," ","-**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.","+**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar, written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention) (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule."," "," **Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":9,"newStart":29,"newLines":9,"lines":[" "," **Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.","+**Why `contested`.** The same paragraph sets the minimum at \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering \"with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load \"by 10-20%\" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), both of which take the load below that minimum; intensive clustering then gives \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). All of these state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them."," ","-A second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].","+A second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409). The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which; see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]."," "," **Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions."," "]},{"oldStart":39,"oldLines":7,"newStart":39,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).","+**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing among its load statements (the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM, the 10-20% strip and 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409)."," "," **Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":33,"oldLines":13,"newStart":33,"newLines":13,"lines":[" "," **Classification inconsistency (not a contradiction).** Maximal eccentrics are described under Maximal Methods in the text, which adds that \"Often the eccentric method is regarded as one of the 'supramaximal' methods\" (PDF p. 407); Fig 7.2 lists eccentrics under supramaximal (PDF p. 406, image). The method is the same; only its heading differs."," ","-**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined.","+**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute. The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). As worded, both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training. PDF p. 406 calls the 1-5 minutes \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\", and the PDF p. 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which, and does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined."," "," **Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]."," "," **When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the 7.5-point width of the circa-maximal band may be no larger than the error of a projected max, so that band may be easier to hit by repetitions in reserve or bar speed than by a percentage. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02)."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency.","+**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group, and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two values that may describe different methods (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency."," "," **Example** — The authors' own examples: partial pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses in the range of the \"sticking point\", with power racks securing the start position (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396); and a weightlifter dropping a near-1RM bar on bumper plates to skip the eccentric phase (PDF p. 407)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":6,"oldLines":7,"newStart":6,"newLines":7,"lines":[" chapter: \"7.1\""," topic: set structures and pyramids"," category: [sets-and-reps, loading, programming, bodybuilding]","-confidence: untested","+confidence: supported"," ingest-tier: full-text"," created: 2026-10-03"," ---"]},{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":9,"newStart":28,"newLines":9,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The one reason the authors give is for stripping and the reverse pyramid: high-repetition sets on the way up are \"too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408), so the repetition work goes after the heavy set. They give no mechanism or study for the other structures."," ","-**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead.","+**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.","+**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block."," "," **Example** — The authors' own maximal-strength pyramid: 6 x 85%, 4 x 90%, 2 x 97%, 1 x 100% of 1RM, with rest lengthening as the load rises, inside 2-6 minutes (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":3,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"617844d6-552d-4d59-906a-569e0ad4e060","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
