{"parentUuid":"393046c8-06a5-4011-88b6-08942f70b2ba","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VmkHoKm13iTKw4V2gUyZyY","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n1\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ec5bdc9e-cac5-4590-97b9-6d626b844393","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:27:55.253Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\n1\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":31,"oldLines":7,"newStart":31,"newLines":7,"lines":[" **Notes beside Fig 7.1** (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, checked against the page image):"," - *Superslow.* Quasi-isometrics \"occurs as a natural consequence of training with near-maximal loads\", but \"there is a discrete method of quasi-isometrics (nowadays popularised in bodybuilding circles as the *superslow method*), which entails performing very slow controlled repetitions with even moderately heavy or lighter loads. In this case the athlete chooses to move the load slowly; with heavier weights he has no option but to move slowly.\" No load, repetition or rest figures are given. The term itself is defined in Chapter 1 ([[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]])."," - *Active stretching.* \"several of the other training methods with heavy resistance used over a full range of movement constitute very effective means of active stretching\".","-- *Connective tissue first.* \"great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues, such as the tendons, ligaments, cartilages and joint capsules, particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases to levels of higher competitive intensity (see 1.6.2)\". Chapter 1 and Chapter 6 hold this point in [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]] and [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]].","+- *Connective tissue first.* \"great emphasis should be placed on increasing the mass and strength of the connective tissues, such as the tendons, ligaments, cartilages and joint capsules, particularly during the early stages of training and transition phases to levels of higher competitive intensity (see 1.6.2)\". Chapter 1 gives the basis (connective tissue adapts more slowly than muscle, [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]) and Chapter 6 the rule ([[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]])."," - *Low-intensity cyclical work.* \"An essential part of the general preparatory phase of all training is also low intensity, prolonged cyclical activity to enhance cardiovascular and circulatory processes, as well as capillarisation in all the relevant muscle groups. This same type of activity can also play a useful role in restoration between intensive training sessions and phases.\" No study, duration or intensity is given."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"393046c8-06a5-4011-88b6-08942f70b2ba","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
