{"parentUuid":"cce193f0-cdca-4483-b5ab-810ba10bf82a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a738c543bcd777bcb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffra5ZEUvYyrxqVSeWHb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I applied all 7 fixes, with small refinements to fixes 4, 5 and 7, and placed all 5 missing ideas without writing a new entry. Lint shows no problems in any 7a file. I edited only 7a files: 7 FIX entries plus the GO chart entry. I made no renames, the scope file is unchanged (10 lines), and I did not touch `_book.md`, 7b, 6d, the vault or git. The log is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7a_applied.md`.\n\n## Per-fix outcome\nI checked each fix against the page image before applying it.\n1. **Maximal methods: applied.** The `contested` reason now covers only the rest between near-maximal singles. It adds the \"optimal recovery intervals between sets\" point (PDF 406) and the note that the two passages may describe different methods. Forced reps no longer appears as an injury-warned method: PDF 407 warns only about eccentrics and cheating. I also made two small consistency edits the review didn't ask for:\n   - \"two incompatible values\" in this entry now says the values \"may describe different methods\".\n   - The interval entry's restatement of the same conflict now says the same.\n2. **Three regimes: applied.** Rating is now `supported`, and the split/zone sentence is labelled Inference.\n3. **Set structures: applied.** Rating is now `supported`. The step-set purpose and \"not for a peaking block\" are labelled Inference.\n4. **Interval and cluster: applied, with one refinement.** The `contested` reason now leads with the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM and the 10-20% strip, then the 75-90%. The review said both fall below the minimum. Only the 6RM end of 4-6RM does, so the entry says \"(a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM)\".\n5. **Hybrid: applied.** The book's 6.6.2 notation shows the superscript is the number of sets:\n   - PDF 335 lists \"60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6\".\n   - PDF 336 writes the same sets as \"10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²\".\n\n   So 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets, and the book's own \"for a set of 3 repetitions\" contradicts its notation. I cite PDF 335-336, not 336 alone. The time/space clause is now labelled Inference.\n6. **Segmental: applied.** The random variant is now used from the start of a block, which is the authors' preventive purpose (PDF 410).\n7. **Contrast: applied, with one refinement.** `contested` now covers only the heavy-light set. Load release and pneumatics are explicitly outside the conflict. Both Russian findings from PDF 175 are quoted word for word with their full citation lists. I did not use the review's \"Western studies that found none\": the same page reports Murray 1959 and Van Huss 1962 as positive. The entry now says the Western studies are split and the Russian studies found a boost.\n\n## Confidence changes\n- **Three regimes: `untested` to `supported`.** The book gives sets, reps, load and rest, calls it \"one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods\", and says the add-ons are \"popularly used\" (PDF 408).\n- **Set structures: `untested` to `supported`.** They have parameters, plateau and stripping are \"commonly used\" (PDF 408), and the pyramid values come from Fig 1.9 equations (PDF 409).\n- **The other eight keep their ratings:**\n  - Hybrid stays `untested`: it gives reps but no load or rest, and the examples are left to \"the inventive coach\".\n  - Segmental stays `untested`: Twenty-Ones gives reps only, the random variant is a novelty the authors name themselves, and Rigert is an anecdote.\n  - Contrast stays `contested`: the conflict on PDF 175 outweighs its parameters.\n  - Chart, miscellaneous and sticking-points stay `untested` for the review's reasons.\n  - Maximal and interval stay `contested`.\n- **Tally:** `untested` 7 to 5, `supported` 0 to 2, `contested` 3 unchanged.\n\n## Missing ideas\nAll five went into the chart entry as quoted text:\n- **Superslow (PDF 405):** no entry anywhere mentions it. It links to `framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology`.\n- **Connective tissue first / low-intensity cyclical work (PDF 405):** connective tissue is already covered, so it links to `claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates` (Ch1 basis) and `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue` (Ch6 rule). No entry covers the cyclical work. I quoted it rather than writing an entry: the book gives it in two sentences with no parameters, so a separate entry would need an invented \"When NOT\" and Example. You can overrule this.\n- **Active stretching (PDF 405):** links to the stretching taxonomy and eccentric quasi-isometrics entries.\n- **Sources and scope (PDF 404 and 414):** a new paragraph, plus a \"When NOT\" line saying the catalogue has not been tested.\n\nFive entries outside 7a could link back to the chart entry; they're listed in the log for you.\n\n## Grep proof (new wording)\n```\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:9:confidence: supported\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:31:Inference: step sets can find\nframework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md:33:Inference: not for a peaking block\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:29:most frequently used in the training of high-level\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:32:superslow method\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:33:very effective means of active stretching\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:34:Connective tissue first\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:35:capillarisation in all the relevant\nframework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md:41:Do not read the catalogue as tested\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:36:optimal recovery intervals between sets\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating\nframework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md:42:two values that may describe different methods\nframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:9:confidence: supported\nframework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md:37:Inference: split routines and zone training\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:The rating applies to the heavy-light set\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:25:the conflict does not reach them directly\nframework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md:29:the Western studies the book cites are split\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:By the 6.6.2 convention\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:21:three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid\nframework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md:27:Inference: also when session time or space\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:32:with one's 4-6RM\" (a 6RM is lighter\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:34:may describe different methods (sets of singles\nframework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md:42:the 10-20% strip and 75-90%\nheuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md:22:from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation\n```\n**Old phrases:** none of the old wording 